<quiz>
  <question type="stack">
    <name>
      <text>Evaluate the Multiple Definite integrals of a function</text>
    </name>
    <questiontext format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[<p>Evaluate the three integrals \(A\) ,\(B\) and \(C\)

<p>\(\displaystyle A =\int_{@limit_1@}^{@limit_2@} ({@f_x@})dx\), \(\displaystyle B =\int_{@limit_1@}^{@limit_3@} ({@f_x@})dx\) ,   \(\displaystyle C = \int_{@limit_3@}^{@limit_2@} ({@f_x@})dx\)</p>

<p>\(A\) = [[input:ans1]] [[validation:ans1]][[feedback:prt1]]</p>
<P>\(B\) = [[input:ans2]] [[validation:ans2]][[feedback:prt2]]</p>
<P>\(C\) = [[input:ans3]] [[validation:ans3]][[feedback:prt3]]</p>
<p>\(B+C = \) [[input:ans4]] [[validation:ans4]][[feedback:prt4]]</p>]]></text>
    </questiontext>
    <generalfeedback format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[<h3>Worked Solution</h3>
<p>Recall that  a definite integral \(f(x)\) with respect to a real variable x on an interval \([a, b]\) is expressed as,\( \displaystyle  \int_ a^bf(x)dx=F(b)-F(a)\), where \(F\) is an antiderivative of \(f\).</p>
<p>Now in order to find \(A\), \(B\) and \(C\) we’ll have,</p>

<p>\(\begin{align} 
\displaystyle A &=\int_{@limit_1@}^{@limit_2@} ({@f_x@})dx\\
&=\displaystyle  \left[ {@coeff_x_0@} x - \frac{x^3}{3}\right]_{{@limit_1@}}^{{@limit_2@}}\\
&=\displaystyle  \left[ {@coeff_x_0@} ({@limit_2@}) - \frac{({@limit_2@})^3}{3}\right]-\left[ {@coeff_x_0@} ({@limit_1@}) - \frac{({@limit_1@})^3}{3} \right] \\
&=\displaystyle  {@coeff_x_0*limit_2-limit_2^3/3@} - {@coeff_x_0*limit_1-limit_1^3/3@} \\
&=\displaystyle  {@ta_1@}\\
\end{align}\)</p>


<p>\(\begin{align} 
\displaystyle B &=\int_{@limit_1@}^{@limit_3@} ({@f_x@})dx\\
&=\displaystyle \left[ {@coeff_x_0@} x - \frac{x^3}{3}\right]_{{@limit_1@}}^{{@limit_3@}}\\
 &=\displaystyle \left[ {@coeff_x_0@} ({@limit_3@}) - \frac{({@limit_3@})^3}{3}\right]-\left[ {@coeff_x_0@} ({@limit_1@}) - \frac{({@limit_1@})^3}{3} \right]\\
&=\displaystyle {@coeff_x_0*limit_3-limit_3^3/3@} - {@coeff_x_0*limit_1-limit_1^3/3@}\\
&= \displaystyle  {@ta_2@}\\
\end{align}\)</p>

<p>
\(\begin{align}
\displaystyle C &=\int_{@limit_3@}^{@limit_2@} ({@f_x@})dx\\
&=\displaystyle \left[ {@coeff_x_0@} x - \frac{x^3}{3}\right]_{{@limit_3@}}^{{@limit_2@}}\\
&=\displaystyle  \left[ {@coeff_x_0@} ({@limit_3@}) - \frac{({@limit_2@})^3}{3}\right]-\left[ {@coeff_x_0@} ({@limit_1@}) - \frac{({@limit_3@})^3}{3} \right]\\
&=\displaystyle  {@coeff_x_0*limit_2-limit_2^3/3@} - {@coeff_x_0*limit_3-limit_3^3/3@}\\
&=\displaystyle  {@ta_3@}
 \end{align}\)
</p>

<p>Using the answers above correctly, the expression \(A=B+C\), because \( \displaystyle {@ta_1@} =\displaystyle {@ta_2@}+\displaystyle {@ta_3@}\)</p>]]></text>
    </generalfeedback>
